
================================================================================
                      THE ANCIENT LIBRARY
================================================================================

You are in a small library.  There are shelves on all
sides of the room, containing many books and knick-knacks.
Some of these shelves look like they haven't been moved
in centuries...

Before you, there is a small table with a very large
book open on it.

================================================================================
                   SEEING WHAT IS HIDDEN: ls -a
================================================================================

Not everything in the dungeon is in plain sight.  Files and
rooms whose names begin with a dot (.) are HIDDEN -- a normal
'ls' will not show them.  To reveal them, add the -a ("all")
flag:

    ls -a            List everything, including hidden names
    ls -la           The same, with details (one per line)

Hidden names you find this way can still be entered:

    cd .somewhere

Keep this spell in mind here -- this library guards a secret.

================================================================================
                   THE ART OF SELF-LEARNING: man
================================================================================

The greatest skill a terminal mage can learn is how to
learn MORE on their own.  The 'man' command opens the
manual page for any command:

COMMAND            PURPOSE
--------           --------
man ls             Read the manual for 'ls'
man grep           Read the manual for 'grep'
man chmod          Read the manual for 'chmod'

Inside a man page, use the same controls as 'less':
    SPACE = next page
    b     = previous page
    /word = search for 'word'
    q     = quit

You can also use --help for a quick summary:

    ls --help
    grep --help

